WEBVTT

-1:59:59.540 --> 00:00:00.000
Hi, Sachin.

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.760
In your introductory comments, you referenced ongoing analysis looking at correlating CDOX to

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.560
outcomes, and that you would use that for the conversation with the regulator. So I guess two

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.040
linked questions. One, will you comment about data when you have it? And B, what conversations

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.140
have you had with the regulator around using that analysis to try and accelerate the buy market

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.860
driven file? Thank you.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:21.140
Yeah.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:28.360
Yeah, Sachin. So we have the previous interactions that Avidity had with FDA

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:34.640
on what would be required in this Phase 1b, Phase 2 study to enable filing.

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:38.020
So we're very clear on what the FDA is looking for.

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:42.500
And, I mean, if you think about it, the way the FDA thinks about this is we know that

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.420
Dux4 is impacted in this disease.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:52.220
How is circulating KHDL-C correlating with Dux4?

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.680
How is that relating to creatine kinase?

00:00:55.680 --> 00:01:02.560
And how is all of this relating ultimately to muscle function as best as we can determine

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:04.720
in the patient set that we have?

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.340
So we have that data.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:13.340
We're analyzing the biopsy data that we have as well from the patients in the trial, and

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.580
then putting that all together to take it to the FDA.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.300
What I can say is the data that we've seen thus far gives us, we believe we have reason

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:24.180
to have the discussion with the FDA and to make the case.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:25.180
We can't.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.380
I can't guarantee that we will win the case, but I think we have what we think is worthy

00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:34.520
of a case that should be made to the FDA for an accelerated filing.

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.860
And once we have that meeting, we'll provide further guidance.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:38.760
Very clear.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:39.160
Thank you.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.120
Thank you.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.860
We will now take our final question for today.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:48.580
And the final question comes from the line of Peter Fadalt from BNP Paribas.

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:49.660
Please go ahead.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.840
Yeah, thanks for the follow-up.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.720
Quick one to end for Mukul.

00:01:54.860 --> 00:01:55.600
Just on Rapsida.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.540
So the Acuvia trends look great, and I heard your comments about it earlier about don't

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:05.280
expect inflection, but can you help us at all giving us a ballpark split between, you

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.080
know, what is bridged versus paid prescriptions right now?

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.600
Just any ballpark numbers would be helpful.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:10.020
Thank you.

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:13.560
Mukul?

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:15.180
Or is it, is this going to be me?

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.460
So I think, well, I can take that, Peter.

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:18.700
I wasn't sure if you were directing it to Mukul.

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:24.500
But, you know, on Rapsida, yeah, we're not providing any detailed guidance on the bridging

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:25.000
program.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.100
You know, what I would say is it's in line with what we're doing.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:33.280
What we've historically seen in terms of getting patients over to paid scripts.

